; ---- Repair archive (the user picks one of three modes) ----
TEXT_TITLE_REPAIR                       = Repair archive
TEXT_REPAIR_INTRO                       = Choose one repair strategy. The original archive is never modified; the result goes to a new file.
TEXT_REPAIR_MODE_1                      = Rebuild the central directory (try this first)
TEXT_REPAIR_MODE_1_HINT                 = Rewrites only the central directory and end record; member data is copied byte for byte, so this is the fastest option. The cost: members whose length is not in the local header (streamed archives) cannot be measured without decompressing, so they are skipped.
TEXT_REPAIR_MODE_2                      = Join split volumes and a truncated tail
TEXT_REPAIR_MODE_2_HINT                 = Reads name.z01 ... name.zip as one stream and allows the last member to be half present: it is written with the bytes that actually exist so the earlier members stay openable. The output is no longer split, because per-volume offsets on a damaged input are not trustworthy.
TEXT_REPAIR_MODE_3                      = Salvage what decodes and repack
TEXT_REPAIR_MODE_3_HINT                 = Decompresses every member for real; whatever decodes is recompressed into a new archive, whatever fails is skipped and named. Slowest, and the new bytes differ from the original - in exchange the central directory and CRCs are recomputed truths.
TEXT_REPAIR_SOURCE                      = Repairing: ${file}
TEXT_REPAIR_OUTPUT                      = Output to:
TEXT_REPAIR_START                       = Start repair
TEXT_REPAIR_FINDINGS                    = What the scan found:
TEXT_REPAIR_HEALTHY                     = The structure of this archive is intact: the central directory and the entry count agree, so there is nothing to repair. An intact structure is not the same as intact data — use Test for that, it decompresses every entry and checks the CRC.
TEXT_REPAIR_HEALTHY_AFTER_TEST           = The skeleton of this archive is intact -- what is damaged is the member data itself. Mode 3 (recover what can be recovered) repacks the members that still read into an archive you can open; whatever those broken bytes held is not coming back.
TEXT_REPAIR_ZIP_ONLY                    = Repair only handles .zip containers: it rebuilds the ZIP skeleton (end record / central directory / local headers) byte by byte. Formats such as 7z or tar have no equivalent structure to rebuild.
TEXT_STATUS_REPAIRING                   = Repairing...
TEXT_STATUS_INSPECTING                  = Checking the archive structure...
TEXT_ERR_REPAIR_FAILED                  = Repair failed:
TEXT_TITLE_REPAIR_RESULT                = Repair result
TEXT_REPAIR_RESULT                      = Recovered ${recovered} member(s), skipped ${skipped}.
TEXT_REPAIR_NOTHING                     = Nothing could be recovered, so no new archive was written; the original is untouched.
TEXT_REPAIR_RECOVERED                   = Recovered
TEXT_REPAIR_SKIPPED                     = Skipped
; Reading the repaired archive back is the point: "recovered N members" is only the repairer's
; own bookkeeping -- whether the file is usable is what the engine says when it reads it.
TEXT_STATUS_VERIFYING                    = Re-checking the repaired archive...
TEXT_REPAIR_RECHECK_PASSED               = Re-check: the whole archive was read and all ${total} member(s) verified.
TEXT_REPAIR_RECHECK_BROKEN               = Re-check: ${bad} of ${total} member(s) still fail verification -- do not treat this file as healthy.
TEXT_REPAIR_RECHECK_SKIPPED              = Not re-checked: 

; ---- Scan for malware (Ctrl+I): asks the local engine only, uploads nothing ----
TEXT_STATUS_SCANNING                    = Scanning...
TEXT_TITLE_SCAN                         = Scan archive for malware
TEXT_SCAN_CLEAN                         = The antimalware engine on this machine looked at the file and reported nothing.
TEXT_SCAN_NOTE                          = It saw this one archive file; whether it opens the container and checks each member is the engine's own decision, and Andzip does not vouch for it. Andzip ships no signature database and never uploads the file anywhere.
TEXT_SCAN_DETECTED                      = The antimalware engine on this machine flagged the file as malicious and blocked it.
TEXT_SCAN_HANDLING                      = What happens next (quarantine, delete, restore) is the engine's call -- look in its protection history. Andzip does not touch your file.
TEXT_SCAN_NO_ENGINE                     = This machine has no usable antimalware engine, so nobody can answer the question.
TEXT_SCAN_NO_ENGINE_HOW                 = Andzip ships no signature database and never sends files to an online scanning service. Turn on Windows Security -> Virus & threat protection, or install any engine that registers with the system scanning interface (AMSI), and this works.
TEXT_SCAN_ENGINE_CHECK                  = What I checked: AMSI init returned ${code}; ${providers} scan providers are registered with Windows; the built-in engine's scanning component is ${oav}.
TEXT_SCAN_OAV_PRESENT                   = present on this machine
TEXT_SCAN_OAV_ABSENT                    = absent from this machine
TEXT_SCAN_NO_ENGINE_WAY_REMOVED          = The built-in engine's scanning layer has been removed from this machine, so Windows Security has nothing left to switch on. To make this work, install an engine that registers with the system scanning interface (AMSI) - putting Defender back counts - then restart. Andzip ships no signature database and never sends files to an online scanning service.
TEXT_SCAN_TOO_BIG                       = This file is over ${limit} MB -- that is the most the engine is handed at once, so nothing was scanned.
TEXT_SCAN_TOO_BIG_NOTE                  = Andzip does not scan a truncated prefix: feeding half the file and reporting "clean" would be a forged result. Scan this file directly in your engine if you need it covered.
TEXT_SCAN_FAILED                        = The engine gave no verdict (${code}). That is neither "clean" nor "bad" -- the question simply went unanswered.

