; ---- Settings: compression page ----
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_TITLE            = Compression settings
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_DEFAULT_LEVEL    = Default compression level
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_LEVEL_HINT       = Which entry the dropdown opens on in "New archive". The first three are about speed, Normal balances speed against ratio, Maximum only cares about ratio.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_NO_ROOT          = Do not create a root folder when compressing a single folder
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_NO_ROOT_HINT     = When on, the archive holds the folder's contents directly instead of the folder itself.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_EXCLUDE_THUMBS   = Exclude Thumbs.db
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_EXCLUDE_TYPES    = File types to exclude when compressing
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_EXCLUDE_HINT     = Semicolon-separated name patterns, e.g. *.bak; *.tmp;. Empty means nothing extra is excluded. Patterns match the file name, not its directory.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_TEST_AFTER       = Test the archive after compression
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_TEST_AFTER_HINT  = Reads every member back out of the archive straight away. It costs one extra pass over the disk and buys you knowing the archive is good now rather than next time you try to open it.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_KEEP_PROGRESS    = Keep the progress window open when compression finishes
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_KEEP_PROGRESS_HINT = When off the progress bar disappears as soon as it completes; when on it stays with the result. Useful for checking the ratio.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_PREALLOC           = Preallocate disk space when compressing
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_PREALLOC_HINT      = Reserve the clusters for the uncompressed total before writing starts. The point is to learn that the disk cannot hold the archive at the beginning instead of at 90%; the cost is that the reservation can be far larger than the finished archive and is held while it runs. Leave off when the target volume is tight.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_SHARE_SOURCES      = Do not lock the files being compressed
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_SHARE_SOURCES_HINT = With it on, other programs can still write to or delete the sources while they are being packed; with it off they have to wait. It does NOT let us read a file another program holds exclusively - Windows refuses that for everyone, backup semantics included. Such a file is skipped and the skipped names are reported when the archive is done.
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_OUTPUT_DIR       = Output folder
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_OUTPUT_LAST      = The folder used for the last new archive
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_OUTPUT_FIXED     = Always output to this folder
TEXT_SETTINGS_COMPRESS_OUTPUT_FIXED_HINT = Leaving it empty behaves like "the folder used for the last new archive".

; ---- Settings: extraction page ----
TEXT_SETTINGS_EXTRACT_TITLE             = Extraction settings
TEXT_SETTINGS_DOUBLE_CLICK_LABEL        = When double-clicking an archive
TEXT_SETTINGS_DOUBLE_CLICK_OPEN         = Open Andzip
TEXT_SETTINGS_DOUBLE_CLICK_EXTRACT      = Smart extract
TEXT_SETTINGS_DOUBLE_CLICK_HINT         = This only changes what the program itself does; it does not rewrite registry verbs, so it takes effect at once for every associated format. Double-clicking then does exactly what the right-click Extract does: read the listing first, then let the shape of the archive decide -- a tree wrapped in one top-level directory lands as that directory, a loose mix is wrapped in a folder named after the archive. Only the progress bar shows, and it closes when it finishes.
TEXT_SETTINGS_EXTRACT_SINGLE_FILE_TO_FOLDER = When extracting, put a lone file into a new folder too
TEXT_SETTINGS_EXTRACT_SINGLE_FILE_HINT  = Off by default: an archive holding one file simply releases that file. When on, a folder named after that file is created (photo.jpg.gz becomes photo\photo.jpg), using "name (2)", "name (3)" when the name is taken. This applies to both the right-click Extract and the double-click Smart extract.
TEXT_SETTINGS_EXTRACT_OPEN_FOLDER       = Open the folder after extraction
